WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.337
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:10.105
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.405 --> 00:00:16.517
Unit four: Public Policy. Today you will announce policy plans with the formal passive future.

4
00:00:16.817 --> 00:00:19.469
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.585
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.590
Legislation.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:29.291
Legislation means laws, considered together.

8
00:00:29.883 --> 00:00:32.684
New legislation on housing was introduced.

9
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:36.806
Your turn. Say: Legislation.

10
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:40.923
Legislation.

11
00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:42.905
Policy maker.

12
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:47.669
Policy maker means a person who helps to decide government plans.

13
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:51.397
Policy makers must balance cost and fairness.

14
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:54.996
Your turn. Say: Policy maker.

15
00:00:57.833 --> 00:00:59.241
Policy maker.

16
00:00:59.983 --> 00:01:01.170
Stakeholder.

17
00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:05.379
Stakeholder means a person or group affected by a decision.

18
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:08.320
Every stakeholder was invited to comment.

19
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:11.418
Your turn. Say: Stakeholder.

20
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:15.291
Stakeholder.

21
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.365
Implementation.

22
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.952
Implementation means the process of putting a plan into action.

23
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.188
Implementation took longer than expected.

24
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:28.563
Your turn. Say: Implementation.

25
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.807
Implementation.

26
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.097
Public consultation.

27
00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:40.312
Public consultation means a process of asking citizens for their views.

28
00:01:40.917 --> 00:01:43.543
A public consultation preceded the new law.

29
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.615
Your turn. Say: Public consultation.

30
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:51.955
Public consultation.

31
00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:53.892
Regulation.

32
00:01:54.883 --> 00:01:58.090
Regulation means an official rule made by a government body.

33
00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:01.414
A new regulation limits plastic packaging.

34
00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:05.087
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

35
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:09.341
Regulation.

36
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:11.468
Impact assessment.

37
00:02:12.483 --> 00:02:16.782
Impact assessment means an official study of the likely effects of a plan.

38
00:02:17.383 --> 00:02:20.230
The impact assessment predicted higher costs.

39
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:23.817
Your turn. Say: Impact assessment.

40
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:28.028
Impact assessment.

41
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:29.564
Reform.

42
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.827
Reform means a change made to improve a system or law.

43
00:02:34.417 --> 00:02:37.078
Tax reform was the main election promise.

44
00:02:37.683 --> 00:02:40.289
Your turn. Say: Reform.

45
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:44.042
Reform.

46
00:02:44.833 --> 00:02:48.171
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.017 --> 00:02:54.804
Visal, a news report says 'The bill is to be debated next week.' What does 'is to be debated' mean?

48
00:02:55.917 --> 00:03:07.369
It is a formal way to announce an official plan: is/are to + be + past participle. It means the debate has been arranged, so it is almost certain.

49
00:03:08.483 --> 00:03:10.390
Do reports use other forms?

50
00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:21.868
Yes: is due to be, is set to be, is expected to be. 'The reform is to be introduced in March' sounds more official than 'will be introduced'.

51
00:03:22.983 --> 00:03:24.646
Can you use it in a negative?

52
00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:36.427
Yes. 'No new regulation is to be adopted until a public consultation has finished.' It often describes orders and rules as well as plans.

53
00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:39.206
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:44.906
Grammar focus: Formal future: is to be and is due to be plus past participle.

55
00:03:45.206 --> 00:03:46.381
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.083 --> 00:03:50.058
The bill is to be debated by the legislature next week.

57
00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:54.378
A public consultation is due to be held before the reform.

58
00:03:55.283 --> 00:03:58.026
The regulation is set to be implemented in March.

59
00:03:58.967 --> 00:04:07.369
Use is / are to be + past participle for official plans and orders. Variants: is due to be, is set to be. It sounds formal and fixed.

60
00:04:08.117 --> 00:04:12.174
Be careful! Don't say: The bill is to debate next week.

61
00:04:14.133 --> 00:04:16.609
Say: The bill is to be debated next week.

62
00:04:16.909 --> 00:04:20.244
Use the passive: is to be + past participle.

63
00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:23.964
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:26.128
From Idea to Law.

65
00:04:26.428 --> 00:04:29.066
Good legislation rarely appears overnight.

66
00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:37.175
First, policy makers identify a problem, and stakeholders such as businesses, unions and families are invited to comment.

67
00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:45.217
A public consultation is to be held before any draft is approved, and an impact assessment is to be published afterwards.

68
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.299
Only then is the regulation set to be implemented, and the reform is to be reviewed after two years.

69
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.656
Each stage allows mistakes to be corrected before they affect millions of people.

70
00:04:57.767 --> 00:05:01.896
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.156
The new law is blank introduced in March.

72
00:05:05.456 --> 00:05:08.622
A, to be. B, to. C, being to.

73
00:05:12.167 --> 00:05:13.702
The answer is A: to be.

74
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.825
A person or group affected by a decision is a blank.

75
00:05:18.125 --> 00:05:22.150
A, regulation. B, stakeholder. C, ballot.

76
00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.071
The answer is B: stakeholder.

77
00:05:29.217 --> 00:05:32.609
An official study of likely effects is an impact blank.

78
00:05:32.909 --> 00:05:36.656
A, campaign. B, reform. C, assessment.

79
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.014
The answer is C: assessment.

80
00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.790
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:52.064
A, The bill is to debate next week.. B, The bill is to be debated next week.. C, The bill is to being debated..

82
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.074
The answer is B: The bill is to be debated next week..

83
00:06:00.217 --> 00:06:03.679
According to the reading, each stage allows mistakes to be blank.

84
00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:07.458
A, corrected. B, hidden. C, repeated.

85
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.918
The answer is A: corrected.

86
00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:17.436
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.283 --> 00:06:22.361
Why is public consultation important before new legislation is passed?

88
00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.674
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:32.974 --> 00:07:01.182
Public consultation gives the people who will live under a law a chance to share their experience before it is final. Policy makers may miss practical problems that stakeholders can see immediately. Listening early improves the quality of legislation, builds trust and makes citizens more willing to accept and follow the new rules, which makes implementation easier.

90
00:07:01.482 --> 00:07:03.215
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:04.117 --> 00:07:11.192
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and formal future: is to be and is due to be plus past participle.

92
00:07:11.492 --> 00:07:16.896
Today's value is civic reasoning. Reason about how power and fairness should work in a society.

93
00:07:17.196 --> 00:07:19.558
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:19.858 --> 00:07:23.172
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

